---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const title = 'UTC offsets explained: UTC+, UTC− and time zones';
const description =
  'What UTC offsets mean, why they change, and why an offset is not the same thing as a time zone.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/utc-offsets-explained">
  <article class="max-w-2xl">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="mb-5 text-xs text-paper-500">
      <a href="/guides" class="ring-focus hover:text-paper-900">Guides</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true" class="mx-1.5">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">UTC offsets</span>
    </nav>
    <header class="mb-8">
      <p class="text-[11px] font-semibold tracking-[0.15em] text-paper-500 uppercase">
        Time-zone basics
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-4xl">
        UTC offsets explained
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        UTC+09:00 tells you a clock is nine hours ahead of Coordinated Universal Time at that
        moment. It does not, on its own, tell you which city the clock belongs to or whether it will
        be nine hours ahead next month.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What “UTC+” and “UTC−” mean</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        UTC is the common reference used to compare civil time around the world. A location at
        <span class="nums"> UTC+02:00</span> is two hours ahead of UTC; a location at
        <span class="nums"> UTC−05:00</span> is five hours behind it. Offsets can include minutes, so
        not every place is separated by whole hours.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">
        An offset is a snapshot; a time zone is a rule set
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        A time zone is a set of rules that determines which offset applies on a particular date.
        <span class="nums"> America/New_York</span>, for example, uses different offsets during
        standard time and daylight saving. Two cities can share today&apos;s offset and still change
        at different dates, or one can change while the other remains fixed. That is why a recurring
        meeting needs cities or IANA zone names rather than a saved offset.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Why abbreviations cause confusion</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Labels such as CST, IST and GMT are convenient in conversation but can be ambiguous or used
        differently in different places. They also encourage people to treat daylight-saving time as
        a separate label instead of a change in the same city&apos;s rules. In an invitation,
        writing a city and date is clearer than relying on an abbreviation alone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-8">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">A practical way to convert safely</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-paper-700">
        Start with the intended date and local time in the city that owns the event. Then convert it
        to each attendee&apos;s city. For a repeating event, check transition dates rather than
        assuming the initial conversion lasts forever. The <a
          href="/"
          class="ring-focus rounded font-medium text-paper-900 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-paper-700"
          >world clock</a
        > and city pages make those date-specific checks easier.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
